@@ -314,3 +315,31 @@ The Eucharist is the fulfillment of the Showbread, known as the Bread of the Pre
 ### 8/24/26, 7:37 PM — Athanasius325 / Fr James
 
 To add to the Shewbread example: The priests were to reverently eat the Bread, and by that ritual they were Communing with God. The Bread was always "in the Presence of God" (Exodus 25:30). The Shewbread was also offered to God, as the Eucharist is offered to God (with us united to the Eucharist; see the Oblation and the arrangement/wording of the 1662 Book of Common Prayer).
+
+---
+
+### 8/25/26, 8:36 AM — JD Smith (OP)
+
+Thanks, that’s helpful. I can see the continuity of imagery there.
+
+What I mean is narrower though. The showbread ceremony itself - twelve loaves set out on the table each Sabbath with frankincense offered with them as Leviticus 24 prescribes - isn’t performed anymore. It didn’t carry on in an adjusted form. It stopped, even if its meaning or imagery carries forward.
+
+What I’m really looking for is what determines whether an Old Testament ceremonial practice ends, or goes on being practiced. 
+
+---
+
+### 8/25/26, 2:16 PM — Athanasius325 / Fr James
+
+The continuation of the practices that we have received take into account the Old Testament practices. The principal of the Shewbread is its signifying the Eucharist. Insofar as we are receiving the Sacrament, we are enacting the fulfillment of what the Shewbread pointed to. 
+
+When it comes to the particular Shewbread practices, we don't do them because those practices aren't what we've received. That's a good enough answer. The onus is upon the innovator who insists that we must have these particular practices done. I am unaware of anyone in church history saying that the reception of the Eucharist should be done the exact way the Shewbread was presented and eaten. 
+
+If a liturgical tradition developed in which the receiving of the Eucharist was done as closely as possible to the way that the Shewbread was done, with the caveat that the Words of Institution were still used and such, then I don't think I'd have any problem with it. I think that to artificially insert it into a particular tradition would be problematic, because it comes across as innovation for innovation's sake. It makes liturgy into a sort of campy form of worship, like liturgical dance in Western liturgy. 
+
+In direct answer to what you're really looking for, what determines whether an Old Testament ceremonial practice ends/goes on is the following in this order: 
+
+1) Scripture
+2) Tradition 
+3) The established customs laid out by the gathered Bishops of a particular sect or jurisdiction
+4) The Bishop Ordinary
+5) The Rector
